  <header class=3D"post-header">
    <h1 class=3D"post-title p-name" itemprop=3D"name headline">Edge River M=
ethod Part 2 of ???: Box Pleating</h1>
    <p class=3D"post-meta">
      <time class=3D"dt-published" datetime=3D"2026-06-17T04:00:00+00:00" i=
temprop=3D"datePublished">Jun 17, 2026
      </time></p>
  </header>

  <div class=3D"post-content e-content" itemprop=3D"articleBody">
   =20

<div style=3D"display:none;">
<mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=3D"CHTML" tabindex=
=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"0" style=3D"font-size: 113.1%; position: relativ=
e;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"></mjx-math><mjx-assist=
ive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3=
.org/1998/Math/MathML"></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>
</div>

<p>In <a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.htm=
l">part 1</a>, I discussed ERM in general, but now it=E2=80=99s time to go =
into a specific application: box pleating,
and specifically the case where the abstraction is made of well-behaved rec=
tangles, which I will call <em>orthogonal box-pleated ERM</em>.
(All cases handled by Soichiro Uchida=E2=80=99s Orixa fall into this case.)=
</p>

<p>First of all, since we=E2=80=99re box pleating, we=E2=80=99re not going =
to be using ideal ERM maps anymore. All rivers must turn 90=C2=B0 at a time=
, except when shifted by a Pythagorean stretch.</p>

<h2 id=3D"river-directions">River Directions</h2>

<p>Let=E2=80=99s revisit an ERM map from the previous post:</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-01-introduction/tadashi-abstraction.svg">
        <figcaption>The abstraction for =E6=AD=A3.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/tadashi-erm.svg">
        <figcaption>The ERM map. Ridges are thin white</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Note that all rivers collapse to either a horizontal line or a vertical =
line<sup id=3D"fnref:line" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=3D"https://www.haya=
stl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fn:line" class=3D"footnote" rel=
=3D"footnote">1</a></sup> in the abstraction, because we=E2=80=99re in orth=
ogonal box-pleated ERM.
We can thus color-code the rivers. There=E2=80=99s actually two ways to col=
or-code the rivers<sup id=3D"fnref:color" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fn:color" class=
=3D"footnote" rel=3D"footnote">2</a></sup>. We can color-code
rivers by target edge direction, or we can color-code rivers by flow direct=
ion. In 2D, this distinction doesn=E2=80=99t matter (you just switch the co=
lors), but
in 3D, it=E2=80=99s a very important distinction. We will call a river that=
 collapses to
a horizontal line a <em>horizontal river</em> or an <em>X-axis river</em> a=
nd a river that collapses to a vertical line a <em>vertical river</em> or a=
 <em>Y-axis river</em>.
We can also name rivers by flow direction, e.g. <em>horizontally-flowing ri=
ver / X-flowing river</em> and <em>vertically-flowing river / Y-flowing riv=
er</em>.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/tadashi-erm-direction.svg">
        <figcaption>The ERM map, with river directions color-coded by targe=
t direction. The stripes are slightly shifted to show where rivers get refl=
ected.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/tadashi-erm-flow-direction.svg">
        <figcaption>The ERM map, with river directions color-coded by flow =
direction. Technically, stripes shouldn't change color along them, but we'r=
e simplifying.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Specifically, in the graphic on the left, yellow rivers are horizontal r=
ivers and purple rivers are vertical rivers.
By the way, in the case of a 3D abstraction (that=E2=80=99s still orthogona=
l), you
end up with 3 different directions:</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-abstraction.svg">
        <figcaption>A 3D abstraction.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>
<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-erm.svg">
        <figcaption>The ERM map (by target direction). Note the 3 river col=
ors.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-erm-flow.svg">
        <figcaption>The ERM map (by flow).</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>To avoid confusion, we won=E2=80=99t call the cyan river a vertical rive=
r (that=E2=80=99s a Y-axis river). We=E2=80=99ll just call it a <em>Z-axis =
river</em>.
By the way, this particular ERM map doesn=E2=80=99t work, but we=E2=80=99ll=
 see why later in the <a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-=
box-pleating.html#3d">3D</a> section.</p>

<p>Separating rivers by direction makes some things clearer and easier to s=
ee, which we=E2=80=99ll get into later. For now, let=E2=80=99s review the E=
RM design process:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Come up with an abstraction. Keep in mind that waffle walls are inevi=
table if there=E2=80=99s an internal point that must be winded more than 36=
0=C2=B0 around.</li>
  <li>Pack the abstraction into a map, forming lakes.</li>
  <li>Send a river from each lake edge and make sure it goes to the sea (pa=
per boundary) or to a lake edge that will end up in the same place.</li>
  <li><em>If two rivers form a concavity, make sure there=E2=80=99s space b=
etween them. Use a peninsula as a reminder.</em></li>
  <li>If two rivers form a convexity, they can intersect as long as distanc=
e constraints are still satisfied. If they cross, beware the river mapping!=
</li>
  <li>Calculate the lakes for river overlaps.</li>
  <li>Draw a crease pattern.</li>
  <li>Profit!</li>
</ol>

<p>We will now go through the steps in detail and see how they apply to box=
 pleating. But note that you don=E2=80=99t just follow these steps in order=
. When checking rivers for concavities,
you may notice that you=E2=80=99ve accidentally created an impossible situa=
tion, and then you need to go back to lake packing to fix it. Or when you g=
o to draw a crease pattern, you may
notice that you can=E2=80=99t resolve a diagonal hinge without it bumping i=
nto a lake, and then realize it=E2=80=99s because you forgot to add space b=
etween concavity-forming rivers, so you need
to go back to river packing<sup id=3D"fnref:concavity" role=3D"doc-noteref"=
><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fn:=
concavity" class=3D"footnote" rel=3D"footnote">3</a></sup>.</p>

<h2 id=3D"making-the-abstraction">Making the Abstraction</h2>

<p>The first step is coming up with an abstraction. Because we=E2=80=99re i=
n orthogonal box-pleated ERM, the abstraction must consist entirely of posi=
tive-integer-dimensions
axis-aligned rectangles. (You can introduce diagonal lines as long as they =
separate lakes and the end result is that all exposed lake edges are grid-a=
ligned.)
The abstraction must be a tree (if there are holes, you need to add slits)
To avoid the need for <a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-=
introduction.html#waffle-walls">waffle walls</a>, you need to make sure tha=
t at no point is it required
to wind more than 360=C2=B0 around a vertex in a closed loop. This can be f=
ixed by adding a slit or by dealing with the waffle walls.</p>

<p>Here are some good abstractions:</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/good-abstraction-trivial.svg">
        <figcaption>A single square. You probably want something more sophi=
sticated though.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/good-abstraction-person.svg">
        <figcaption>A more sophisticated abstraction. There's distortion at=
 the bottom to show a slit, but all shapes are indeed rectangles.</figcapti=
on>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/good-abstraction-hidden-corner.svg">
        <figcaption>A hidden corner is included. There's a 45=C2=B0 diagona=
l line, but since all exposed lake edges are grid-aligned, it's okay.</figc=
aption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 250px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/good-abstraction-overlap.svg">
        <figcaption>It looks like there's holes in this abstraction, but th=
at's just overlap. Distortion shows that this abstraction is indeed a tree.=
</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/good-abstraction-brick.svg">
        <figcaption>This abstraction is also a tree despite having internal=
 vertices. The condition is no holes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/good-abstraction-corner-wall.svg">
        <figcaption>A good 3D abstration. The central vertex can be winded =
