<h2 id=3D"packing-the-lakes">Packing the Lakes</h2>

<p>Once we have a nice abstraction, we need to pack the lakes onto a box-pl=
eated grid. If we want axial creases to stay on the grid, an important rule=
 to follow is the
<em>parity rule for box pleating</em>: <em>the taxicab distance (horizontal=
 distance + vertical distance) between any two paper points that map to the=
 same abstraction point is even</em>.
This is because pleats have integer width, so they shift paper by an even w=
idth. If you want to shift paper by an odd width, you need a half-integer w=
ide pleat.</p>

