<h2 id=3D"sending-the-rivers">Sending the Rivers</h2>

<p>Next up is sending a river from each lake edge that either goes to the s=
ea or goes to a lake edge that ends up in the same place. We=E2=80=99re in =
box pleating, so box pleating rules
should be followed here. That means 90=C2=B0 turns only, except at Pythagor=
ean stretches.</p>

<p>One thing special about box pleating is that since all river directions =
are parallel or perpendicular to each other, you don=E2=80=99t have to thin=
k about backside lake placement as much,
and you can simply send a river from a terminating lake edge (one that=E2=
=80=99s not supposed to connect to another lake)
to the terminating lake edge on the opposite side (and a backside lake will=
 magically appear).</p>

<p>To illustrate this, we will draw a <em>blockade map</em>, which is a map=
 where no rivers are allowed to go to the sea<sup id=3D"fnref:blockade" rol=
e=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-=
pleating.html#fn:blockade" class=3D"footnote" rel=3D"footnote">4</a></sup>.=
</p>

