<h2 id=3D"spacing-the-concavities"><em>Spacing the Concavities</em></h2>

<p>Now for a very important part of ERM: making sure that rivers that form =
a concavity have a peninsula between them. Let=E2=80=99s analyze the box-pl=
eating case. Recall that in
box-pleating, the minimum pleat width is 1 unit, and the distance between r=
ivers is usually<sup id=3D"fnref:box-usually" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=
=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fn:box-usua=
lly" class=3D"footnote" rel=3D"footnote">6</a></sup> an integer. Since we k=
now from=20
<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/08/erm-01-introduction.html#peni=
nsulas">part 1</a> that rivers forming a concavity aren=E2=80=99t allowed t=
o touch, the smallest distance between concavity-forming rivers is 1
unit. And in fact, 1 unit is enough:</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/gamma-abstraction.svg">
        <figcaption>A concavity.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/gamma-erm-cp.svg">
        <figcaption>A crease pattern (with a partial ERM map in the backgro=
und, peninsula not drawn) showing that 1 unit of separation is enough</figc=
aption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/gamma-movement.svg">
        <figcaption>The concavity-forming rivers go back and forth between =
these two pictures. Note that the maximum distance is 1.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>One caveat that you can see above is that because of the parity rule, if=
 rivers alternate mountain and valley with width-1 pleats, rivers separated=
 by an odd distance
will have their MV assignments misaligned, requiring a long reverse fold, w=
hich ends up acting like a spring when folded, for better or worse.
If you want to avoid those, you need to use some other MV pattern or separa=
te all rivers by even distances, and in the case
of concavity-forming rivers, since they can=E2=80=99t be separated by 0, th=
ey must be separated by at least 2.</p>

<p>Now, this rule is just for a 90=C2=B0 concavity. For a 180=C2=B0 concavi=
ty (i.e. a slit), you may think you can do the same thing as for a 90=C2=B0=
 concavity:</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
