    </figure>
</div>

<p>This, in particular, means that you cannot cross a (positive-width) rive=
r through a river that already connects two edges that go to the same place=
, unless you then
proceed to cross it back.</p>

<p>I=E2=80=99m not aware of any valid crossing overlaps that aren=E2=80=99t=
 the perpendicular cross, so let=E2=80=99s move on to the more complicated =
type:</p>

<h3 id=3D"hugging-overlaps">Hugging Overlaps</h3>

<p>In a <em>hugging overlap</em>, two rivers hug each other, and then separ=
ate. In ideal ERM, the far banks of the rivers (the ones not intersecting)
can=E2=80=99t get closer to each other than their corresponding points are =
in the final model. In other words, the minimum width of the hugging overla=
p (the <em>hug width</em>) must not be shorter
than the final distance between the two points that the far banks correspon=
d to. The box pleating condition is more complicated, and I still don=E2=80=
=99t quite know it, but
this rule is a good starting point.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/hug.svg">
        <figcaption><span>Examples of valid hugging overlaps.<sup id=3D"fnr=
ef:hug" role=3D"doc-noteref"><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/=
erm-02-box-pleating.html#fn:hug" class=3D"footnote" rel=3D"footnote">9</a><=
/sup> The hug width is at least the distance between the corresponding poin=
ts (in these cases, <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" ja=
x=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"27" style=3D"font-size: 117.=
4%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><=
mjx-mn class=3D"mjx-n"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c32"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-msqrt=
><mjx-sqrt><mjx-surd><mjx-mo class=3D"mjx-n"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c221A"></m=
jx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-surd><mjx-box style=3D"padding-top: 0.174em;"><mjx-mn c=
lass=3D"mjx-n"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-c32"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-box></mjx-sq=
rt></mjx-msqrt></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D=
"inline"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mn>2</mn><msqr=
t><mn>2</mn></msqrt></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>).</span></f=
igcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/bad-hug.svg">
        <figcaption>An invalid hugging overlap. The hug width is less than =
the distance between the corresponding points, so the paper will stretch.</=
figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>The simplest hugging overlap is the <em>perpendicular hug</em>, where tw=
o rivers hug and then immediately turn 90=C2=B0 away from each other, as in=
 the example on the left. This
forces the following lakes if the rivers have the same width and hug as clo=
se as possible (which we will call a
<em>complete perpendicular hug</em>):</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/perpendicular-hug-lakes.svg">
        <figcaption>The lakes formed by a complete perpendicular hug.</figc=
aption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>A perpendicular hug that is not complete (either the rivers don=E2=80=99=
t hug as close as possible, or they=E2=80=99re not even the same width), ca=
n be filled in similarly, but
it is not forced. Let=E2=80=99s take a look at an incomplete perpendicular =
hug and the ways it can be turned into lakes.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/incomplete-perp-hug.svg">
        <figcaption>An incomplete perpendicular hug. The rivers don't have =
the same width.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/incomplete-perp-lakes.svg">
        <figcaption><span>Ways to turn an incomplete perpendicular hug into=
 lakes. And there=E2=80=99s intermediate ones as well.
        We let a river seem to run into a diagonal lake edge for simplicity=
.</span></figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Of course, there=E2=80=99s no reason to use any of them other than the o=
ne on the left, which is the simplest one.</p>

<p>The other type we=E2=80=99ll talk about is the <em>parallel hug</em>, wh=
ere two rivers hug each other for a distance and then separate away. These =
tend to require awkward diagonal lines
to fill in. And because these overlaps can actually last a while, and since=
 the rivers end up agreeing everywhere on where the creases should go, perh=
aps
the standard strategy of replacing overlapping rivers with lakes isn=E2=80=
=99t the best idea for showing river flow:</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/parallel-hug.svg">
        <figcaption>A parallel hug. This is the same as the example shown s=
omewhere above.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/parallel-hug-lakes.svg">
        <figcaption>One way to turn the overlap into lakes. Oops, infinite =
lakes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>We would like to instead treat this like a river of length 3, but we can=
=E2=80=99t, because it=E2=80=99s composed of two rivers that <em>flow</em> =
in different directions
(<em>flow direction</em>, not target direction, though this only matters in=
 3D). You usually
don=E2=80=99t have a diagonal hinge cutting through the heart of a river li=
ke that. So we=E2=80=99ll just revert back to overlapping river notation fo=
r the parallel part of the overlap:</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/parallel-hug-lakes-2.svg">
        <figcaption>The notation we'll use when we want to show how paralle=
l overlaps get filled with lakes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>One caveat about this particular example is that the rivers are separate=
d by -1, which is an odd number, so expect that diagonal hinge to instead b=
e a long reverse fold
if you fill rivers with alternating mountain and valley. It=E2=80=99s like =
the shadow version of rivers separated by 1 to secure a concavity. Of cours=
e, you can make parallel
hugs with even thicker overlaps as long as the hug width doesn=E2=80=99t de=
crease too much.</p>

<p>For hugging overlaps, I=E2=80=99m pretty sure I know the distance rule<s=
