<p>Let=E2=80=99s take a look at a valid separation by -2, since those can g=
et wonky. The example we=E2=80=99ll use is a scaled-up version of an alread=
y seen example, but this time the scaling
is valid since that example already followed the even-separation case of th=
e hug width rule (though it only needed to follow the odd-separation case).=
</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/width-2-hug.svg">
        <figcaption>A valid parallel hug with separation -2.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Now let=E2=80=99s fill it with lakes. There=E2=80=99s a tiny bit of slac=
k, so there=E2=80=99s multiple ways to do this. Unfortunately, you don=E2=
=80=99t get a nice 45=C2=B0 diagonal line because that only
works for separation -1.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/width-2-hug-lakes.svg">
        <figcaption>A way to fill in the lakes. Diagonal creases on the ove=
rlap are emphasized.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>The bad news is that you have awkward diagonal creases on the parallel p=
art. The good news is that those diagonal creases don=E2=80=99t need to be =
reverse folds since
the separation is even. Anyway, as a fun fact, we learned that the smallest=
 (by hug width) case of a parallel hug is width-1 hugging width-1 with sepa=
ration -1, but
that takes advantage of the slack offerend by the odd-separation case of th=
e hug width rule. The smallest case that follows the even-separation case i=
s
width-2 hugging width-2 with separation -1. The smallest case with even sep=
aration is width-4 hugging width-3 with separation -2.</p>

<p>So yeah, overlapping the convexities. Perpendicular crosses are sometime=
s required, and all other overlaps are just to save space. And overlaps ten=
d to turn into lakes, with
all that comes with.</p>

<h2 id=3D"calculating-the-lakes">Calculating the Lakes</h2>

<p>We touched quite a bit on this step in the previous section, but here, y=
ou take those overlaps, turn them into lakes, and deal with the consequence=
s. As a review:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Perpendicular cross: turns into a rectangle-shaped lake.</li>
  <li>Perpendicular hug (complete or incomplete): turns into a couple of ri=
ght-isosceles-triangle-shaped lakes. Incomplete ones have other patterns; d=
on=E2=80=99t use them.</li>
  <li>Parallel hug: requires a weird Pythagorean shift (unless it=E2=80=99s=
 separation -1), and spits out some number of diagonal hinges that the othe=
r side of the overlap eats up.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id=3D"drawing-the-creases">Drawing the Creases</h2>

<p>Now it=E2=80=99s time for the fun part. You take the ERM map that you=E2=
=80=99ve worked so hard on and figure out why in the world the creases aren=
=E2=80=99t lining up right. And you then realize that
you=E2=80=99ve ignored parity when placing a lake. Or when overlapping rive=
rs. Or you forgot to separate concavity-forming rivers. Or you created this=
 nice beautiful overlap, but
because it happens too close to a hidden edge of a lake, it technically bre=
aks the paper distance rule. And you have to go back, fix your ERM map, and=
 even realize that it=E2=80=99s just
impossible with the current grid size. Oh well. But you won=E2=80=99t reali=
ze any of that until you actually draw some creases.</p>

<p>Before we draw creases, we need to select certain lake edges to be <em>h=
idden</em>. Let=E2=80=99s say we want to make a nice cute concave =CE=93, a=
nd we=E2=80=99ve made a nice ERM map for it:</p>

