<p>The threatened partial is magically resolved with a reverse fold! In the=
 case of rivers separated by 1 to secure a concavity, if you alternate moun=
tain and valley on the rivers,
that reverse fold is specifically a valley. You can use this fact to predic=
t the direction of the reverse fold. There=E2=80=99s also the shadow versio=
n of this: in the case of
rivers separated by -1 due to a parallel hug, there=E2=80=99s a mountain re=
verse fold.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 800px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/parallel-hug-creases.svg">
        <figcaption>Crease pattern for the parallel hug example. Note the m=
ountain reverse fold on the right.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<h3 id=3D"problems">Problems</h3>

<p>Problems can arise when you=E2=80=99re trying to resolve hinges. Sometim=
es, like the above example of =CE=93 with a long top edge, it=E2=80=99s jus=
t because there=E2=80=99s rivers separated by an
odd number of units, making the hinge resolution trickier. But sometimes, t=
here=E2=80=99s an actual problem with the ERM map.</p>

<p>Sometimes you can have threatened partials that don=E2=80=99t resolve, i=
n which case, check your lake position parity. If it=E2=80=99s wrong, you g=
otta go all the way back to
<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#pack=
ing-the-lakes">Packing the Lakes</a>.</p>

<p>Sometimes you might have a hinge that seems to have nowhere to go other =
than directly through a lake. In that case, make sure you=E2=80=99re separa=
ting your concavity-forming rivers,
as this is a common symptom of not doing so. If you didn=E2=80=99t, you hav=
e to go back to <a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pl=
eating.html#spacing-the-concavities">Spacing the Concavities</a>.</p>

<p>Sometimes you might have a hinge that seems to have nowhere to go other =
than on top of a hidden edge. In this case, it=E2=80=99s probably because y=
ou=E2=80=99re overlapping rivers in such
a way that the lakes that they form stretch the paper under the assumption =
that the hidden edge is a hidden edge. This is a more interesting problem, =
so let=E2=80=99s analyze a case:</p>

