a 135=C2=B0 angle. Then there=E2=80=99s only one way to close the vertex, w=
hich is to make the rest of the land there red. A similar argument applies =
at the midpoint of <mjx-container class=3D"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0" jax=
=3D"CHTML" tabindex=3D"0" ctxtmenu_counter=3D"118" style=3D"font-size: 117.=
4%; position: relative;"><mjx-math class=3D"MJX-TEX" aria-hidden=3D"true"><=
mjx-mover><mjx-over style=3D"padding-bottom: 0.18em; margin-bottom: -0.248e=
m;"><mjx-mo class=3D"mjx-n"><mjx-stretchy-h class=3D"mjx-c2013" style=3D"wi=
dth: 1.396em;"><mjx-ext><mjx-c></mjx-c></mjx-ext></mjx-stretchy-h></mjx-mo>=
</mjx-over><mjx-base><mjx-mrow><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-=
c1D443 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mi class=3D"mjx-i"><mjx-c class=3D"mjx-=
c1D446 TEX-I"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-mrow></mjx-base></mjx-mover></mjx-math=
><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=3D"on" display=3D"inline"><math xmlns=3D"h=
ttp://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><mover><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow>=
<mo accent=3D"true">=E2=80=95</mo></mover></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-=
container>.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-planes-partial-3.svg">
        <figcaption>Filling in some of the land on the top right. Note the =
forced 135=C2=B0 angle.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>Now we just have to deal with the center point of the map. Thinking abou=
t where the rivers go, the land must be red, and the diagonal creases must =
go in specific
places to prevent the center vertex from being winded more than 360=C2=B0.<=
/p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-planes-complete.svg">
        <figcaption>The complete plane coloring.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>I may have went through this a little quickly, but there=E2=80=99s a lot=
 of visualizing that has to go into this process, and you need to think abo=
ut where hidden edges
will end up at the same time, so it=E2=80=99s pretty involved and would tak=
e a while to explain. But now that we have a complete plane map, here=E2=80=
=99s an ERM map, complete
with filled-in lands and hidden edges.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-erm-4.svg">
        <figcaption>The resulting ERM map, with hidden edges marked. Looks =
pretty complicated.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>You might notice that there=E2=80=99s some lakes without rivers coming o=
ut of them. In box pleating (and general finite-crease origami), there=E2=
=80=99s this principle that, actually
every point is either part of a lake or the boundary of a lake, and in box =
pleating specifically, whereever there=E2=80=99s a river, there=E2=80=99s a=
lways a perpendicular river running
though it creating a perpendicular cross and thus a lake. These perpendicul=
ar rivers get reflected a lot.
We don=E2=80=99t draw the perpendicular rivers to keep the map simple. But =
here, these extra lakes are formed by a perpendicular hug of perpendicular =
rivers, so let=E2=80=99s draw
in the perpendicular rivers to illustrate the principle.</p>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-erm-perp.svg">
        <figcaption>Perpendicular rivers have been drawn in.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <img style=3D"max-width: 400px;" src=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/ass=
ets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-erm-perp-2.svg">
        <figcaption>The perpendicular rivers perpendicular hug to form a co=
uple of lakes.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>A fun fact is that the target direction of the perpendicular river is th=
e same as the flow direction of the original river and vice versa.</p>

<p>Anyway, now let=E2=80=99s try turning this one into a crease pattern.</p=
>

<div class=3D"figrow">
    <figure>
        <div>
<img style=3D"min-width: 250px; max-width: 250px;" src=3D"https://www.hayas=
tl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-2-rivers.fold.d.svg" cl=
ass=3D"fold">
<div class=3D"fold-options" style=3D"display: none;">
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-rivers.fold">.fold</a></div>
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-rivers.fold.cp">.cp</a></div>
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-rivers.fold.svg">.svg</a></div>
</div>
</div>
        <figcaption>Filling in the river creases.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <div>
<img style=3D"min-width: 250px; max-width: 250px;" src=3D"https://www.hayas=
tl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-2-hinges.fold.d.svg" cl=
ass=3D"fold">
<div class=3D"fold-options" style=3D"display: none;">
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-hinges.fold">.fold</a></div>
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-hinges.fold.cp">.cp</a></div>
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-hinges.fold.svg">.svg</a></div>
</div>
</div>
        <figcaption>Resolving the easy-to-resolve incomplete vertices. Some=
times creases have to flip direction.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
        <div>
<img style=3D"min-width: 250px; max-width: 250px;" src=3D"https://www.hayas=
tl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/corner-wall-2-90.fold.d.svg" class=
=3D"fold">
<div class=3D"fold-options" style=3D"display: none;">
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-90.fold">.fold</a></div>
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-90.fold.cp">.cp</a></div>
<div><a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/assets/posts/erm-02-box-pleating/co=
rner-wall-2-90.fold.svg">.svg</a></div>
</div>
</div>
        <figcaption>Using what we know about the structure to fill in the 9=
0=C2=B0 creases.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</div>

<p>And we did it! Making a 3D model requires a lot of thinking about struct=
ure, and really making sure the points are winded 360=C2=B0. And there=E2=
=80=99s often times when you want
a specific layer ordering but it won=E2=80=99t work with the specific layou=
t you have (thankfully we were lucky this time). If you=E2=80=99re beginnin=
g with ERM, I=E2=80=99d recommend sticking to 2D,
or at least to 3D models that can be folded flat.</p>

<h2 id=3D"profit">Profit!</h2>

<p>We=E2=80=99ve made it, the final step of the ERM design process. You mad=
e an ERM map, and it=E2=80=99s excellent. You drew the crease pattern, and =
it folds flat (or into the intended 3D shape)
with the correct layer ordering. This is the final step: you get out a real=
 piece of paper, and actually fold the pattern. There are steps you can tak=
e to make the collapse
easier, but that=E2=80=99s a topic for another post.</p>

<h2 id=3D"summary">Summary</h2>

<p>Now you=E2=80=99re ready to start designing with orthogonal box-pleated =
ERM! This post was long and full of pretty pictures, so here=E2=80=99s a su=
mmary of the process:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Come up with an abstraction.
    <ul>
      <li>All exposed lake edges should be grid-aligned.</li>
      <li>No holes.</li>
      <li>No winding vertices more than 360=C2=B0 unless you want waffle wa=
lls.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Pack the abstraction into a map, forming lakes.
    <ul>
      <li>The distance between two points on the paper corresponding to the=
 same point in the abstraction should be even.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Send a river from each lake edge and make sure it goes to the sea (pa=
per boundary) or to a lake edge that will end up in the same place.
    <ul>
      <li>Rivers are all parallel or perpendicular to each other&gt;</li>
      <li>Draw a blockade map if you don=E2=80=99t know how constraints are=
 satisfied in the sea.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>
<em>If two rivers form a concavity, make sure there=E2=80=99s space between=
 them. Use a peninsula as a reminder.</em>
    <ul>
      <li>Don=E2=80=99t forget this!</li>
      <li>1 unit of separation is enough for a 90=C2=B0 concavity.</li>
      <li>For concavities with more degrees, split them and use width-0 riv=
ers.</li>
      <li>Concavity-forming rivers can touch at a vertex.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>If two rivers form a convexity, they can intersect as long as distanc=
e constraints are still satisfied. If they cross, beware the river mapping!
    <ul>
      <li>The parity rule of lakes applies here too.</li>
      <li>Perpendicular crosses become rectangle-shaped lakes.</li>
      <li>Perpendicular hugs become a couple of right-isosceles-triangle-sh=
aped lakes.</li>
      <li>Remember the hug width rule for parallel hugs. They tend to becom=
e lakes with weird diagonal lines.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Calculate the lakes for river overlaps.
    <ul>
      <li>Do this while thinking about overlapping convexities.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Draw a crease pattern.
    <ul>
      <li>Mark hidden edges.</li>
      <li>Fill in rivers with axial creases.</li>
      <li>Resolve incomplete vertices with hinges or reverse folds.</li>
      <li>3D is hard and requires a lot of visualizing.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Profit!
    <ul>
      <li>Fold it!</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ol>

<p>A nice exercise would be to take the =E6=AD=A3 model at the very top and=
 make it more efficient. It=E2=80=99s currently on a 23 grid, but perhaps i=
t can become a 20 grid instead.
(19 is impossible without a belt because then there=E2=80=99s simply not en=
ough perimeter for everything to escape to the sea, and this doesn=E2=80=99=
t seem like a model that requires
a belt. With 20, there=E2=80=99s enough perimeter, but you can only afford =
to let 2 units of perimeter go to waste, so maybe it=E2=80=99s possible, ma=
ybe it=E2=80=99s not.)</p>

<div class=3D"footnotes" role=3D"doc-endnotes">
  <ol>
    <li id=3D"fn:line" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>more like a thin strip, since the rivers are discretized.&nbsp;<a =
href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fnref:l=
ine" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:color" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>I was almost done with this post when a 3D map made me realize tha=
t distinguishing the 2 ways to color code rivers can actually be important.=
 Oh well.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-ple=
ating.html#fnref:color" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=
=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:concavity" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>There=E2=80=99s a reason why that step is emphasized. It=E2=80=99s=
 the most common reason why I have to abandon a grid size and increase it.&=
nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html=
#fnref:concavity" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9=
</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:blockade" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>A blockade map helps with tracking where the rivers are supposed t=
o go so you don=E2=80=99t accidentally cross rivers that shouldn=E2=80=99t =
cross. In addition, a blockade map is basically necessary if your design re=
quires the part in question to consist entirely of middle flaps.&nbsp;<a hr=
ef=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fnref:blo=
ckade" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:advance" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>At this point a certain ERM designer might say =E2=80=9Cswamp!=E2=
=80=9D, but shh, this is different.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com=
/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fnref:advance" class=3D"reversefootnot=
e" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:box-usually" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>even with Pythagorean stretches. However, partials can break this.=
&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.htm=
l#fnref:box-usually" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=
=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:local" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>which may actually just be fine, since it=E2=80=99s very local.&nb=
sp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#f=
nref:local" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></=
p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:perpendicular-required" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>potentially, because they=E2=80=99re not required if the backside =
lakes are explicitly drawn.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06=
/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fnref:perpendicular-required" class=3D"reverse=
footnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:hug" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>There=E2=80=99s some space between the rivers in the left one for =
clarity. It=E2=80=99s not necessary, and it=E2=80=99s not even necessary to=
 separate the intersection from the lake.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayas=
tl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fnref:hug" class=3D"reversefootn=
ote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:distance" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>maybe the transition to the parallel hug may be weird, but other t=
han that.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-ple=
ating.html#fnref:distance" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=
=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:boundaries" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>shh!&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box=
-pleating.html#fnref:boundaries" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-back=
link">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:RQ" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>This is fine despite that segment being in the middle of a lake, b=
ecause that river=E2=80=99s going to be hidden. Rivers can correspond to se=
gments in the middle of lakes.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026=
/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fnref:RQ" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D=
"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:corner-wall" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>I=E2=80=99m aware that there=E2=80=99s a <a href=3D"https://erikde=
maine.org/papers/MazeFolding_Origami5/paper.pdf">paper about folding mazes<=
/a>. Shh!&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.hayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-ple=
ating.html#fnref:corner-wall" class=3D"reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlin=
k">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
    <li id=3D"fn:plane-color" role=3D"doc-endnote">
      <p>You may notice that I=E2=80=99ve been using the secondary colors o=
f light for river colors (a.k.a directions), so it=E2=80=99s only natural t=
o use the primary colors of light for planes.&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www.h=
ayastl.com/2026/06/17/erm-02-box-pleating.html#fnref:plane-color" class=3D"=
reversefootnote" role=3D"doc-backlink">=E2=86=A9</a></p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</div>

